## Doctrine 10

**A customer deliverable is produced by a versioned playbook and an eval —
never by a person improvising in a session.** *(WSJF prioritization pass,
2026-08-05.)* The first engagements are human-assisted, which makes it
cheapest in the moment to just do the work in a session and hand the output
over. Do that twice and the company is an agency: the value lives in the
operator, nothing compounds, and the runtime gets built later against
remembered requirements instead of recorded ones. Every artifact a customer
receives must trace to a committed playbook and a gate that was made to go
red. Then productization is *extraction* from something that already works,
and the services phase is paid requirements-gathering for the runtime rather
than a detour around it. See `docs/MVP-WSJF-2026-08-05.md`.

## Doctrine 21

**An acceptance criterion that greps source text for a literal is
self-invalidating. Assert structure structurally.** *(W-49 retro, 2026-08-09
— two instances in one iteration.)* Doctrine 7's W-28 amendment says to run a
criterion before the lock. W-49 did. **Both criteria still broke**, because
running one proves it is *true now*, not that it is *stable under the
implementation it was written to check*.

* **AC-7** was `grep -c 'workflow_dispatch' <workflow> → 1`. It genuinely
  returned `1` and was executed pre-lock. Then the implementation added a
  comment block *explaining why `workflow_dispatch` was retained* — count
  became `2`. This is F-17's AC-12 defect (doctrine 7) reproduced verbatim,
  in the same session in which that doctrine was read.
* **AC-11** was `grep -c '<stale instruction>' <runbook> → 0`. The
  implementation rewrote the runbook and added a note explaining what the
  instruction *used to say* — quoting it word-for-word. Count: `1`.

The pattern: **a criterion that scans prose is broken by the prose written to
satisfy it.** The better a change is documented, the more likely its own
criterion fails. Running it pre-lock cannot detect this; the invalidating
text does not exist yet.

**The fix is not "grep more cleverly" and not "stop writing comments."**
Where the thing being asserted has structure — a workflow's trigger set, a
JSON key, a YAML step order, a package `bin` — parse it and assert the parsed
value. W-49's replacement asserted `sorted(on.keys())` and step *indices*, and
is immune to any amount of surrounding commentary. Reserve text greps for
things that genuinely are text.

**And when a text grep IS right, repair in the correct direction — it is not
always the criterion.** W-49's two cases went opposite ways, and getting this
backwards silently removes a real control:

| | What the literal was | Right repair |
|---|---|---|
| AC-7 | a keyword appearing in a comment — harmless | fix the **criterion** (parse it) |
| AC-11 | a stale *instruction* sitting in a runbook — someone will grep, find it, and follow it | fix the **document** |

Ask what the match *means*. If a reader hitting that text would be misled,
the document is the defect and the criterion just did its job. If they would
not, the criterion was measuring the wrong thing. "My grep matched my own
comment" invites one reflex; it was the wrong one half the time here.

**The same brittleness applies to counts in the acceptance checklist.**
*(W-16 retro, 2026-08-09.)* F-30's checklist said the unit suite returns
"33 pass". It returns 30 — all passing, no criterion unmet, but the number
was written at the end of the session and never executed, and the acceptance
gate caught it by running it. A checklist is the last artifact written and
the one most likely to carry an asserted number. Two habits fix it: name the
**command and its stable pass condition** (`→ 0 fail`) rather than a count
that any future test addition invalidates, and treat any number you type
into a checklist as a toolchain claim that must come from a command you just
ran (doctrine 7's W-28 amendment, which this violated *inside the checklist
whose purpose is catching it*).

**The sharpening — "assert structure structurally" is not enough when the
STRUCTURE DOES NOT EXIST YET.** *(W-27 retro, 2026-08-11 — three instances
in one item, by the session that wrote the warning.)* F-35's acceptance
section opens by citing this doctrine and promising every criterion asserts
structure rather than prose. Three then broke anyway (AC-1 a heading count,
AC-4 an absence-of-a-token, AC-6 a link convention the file does not use),
and the pattern separating them from the ones that held is exact:

| Criterion | Asserted against | Held? |
|---|---|---|
| AC-2/AC-3 step indices | `commands/loop.md`, **already existed** | yes |
| AC-1 heading count | a playbook **not yet written** | no — 7 vs 9 |
| AC-6 link style | a list style **not looked at** | no — code spans, not links |

A structural assertion about an artifact that does not exist yet is not a
measurement, it is **a guess about your own future design** wearing a
parser's authority. AC-1 said 7 headings; the finished playbook needed two
unnumbered appendices, so the count was wrong the moment the file got good.

Two fixes, both cheap. **Anchor criteria to artifacts that already exist**
wherever possible — indices in a file you can open today are stable in a way
counts in a file you have not written are not. Where the artifact is new,
assert an **invariant you control** (`the numbered sections are 1..n, in
order`, which survives any appendix) rather than a **quantity you predicted**.

And the meta-lesson, which is the uncomfortable one: reading a doctrine
immediately before doing the work does not prevent the doctrine's failure.
All three of these were written minutes after quoting doctrine 21 in the
same document. Prose in your own context is not a control either — the same
thing doctrine 12 says about subagent prompts, pointed at yourself.

**Mechanical fix for the commonest shape: an ABSENCE assertion over source
must strip comments before scanning.** *(W-42 retro, 2026-08-15 — three
instances in one item, which makes it a pattern rather than three slips.)*
Every criterion of the form "token X does not appear in file Y" was broken
by the comment written to document the property it asserts:

| Criterion | Matched its own | Count |
|---|---|---|
| AC-1 "the adjudicator is pure" | `// No fetch. No node:fs. No process.env.` | 1, wanted 0 |
| AC-3 "no judge bypass exists" | `// …the operator remembers to pass --judge` | 1, wanted 0 |
| AC-17 "no artifact still says the gap is open" | the spec's own quotation of the sentence | 2, wanted 0 |

The direction of the correlation is the point: **the better you document a
property, the more certainly its own absence-check fails.** So an item that
writes careful comments is the item most likely to ship a broken criterion,
and "grep more cleverly" scales badly because each author rediscovers it.

The repo already had the answer and nobody reused it —
`scripts/collabb-new/tests/run.sh` AC-12 strips comment lines first, and its
own comment records that anchoring on raw text was tried and rejected
(doctrine 38, unlearned). W-42 shipped `scan_code()` in
`scripts/redact-judge-mutations.sh` doing the same for `.mjs`. **Before
writing any absence-over-source criterion, look for an existing
comment-stripping scanner and use it; if you write a new one, expect the
next item to need it too.**

## Doctrine 38

**Before writing an acceptance criterion for an invariant, grep for whether the
invariant is already asserted. Re-inventing a control silently downgrades it.**
*(W-34 retro, 2026-08-12.)* Doctrine 21 is about criteria that are *brittle*.
This one was worse and in the opposite direction: it was **weaker than the check
that already shipped**.

F-40's AC-15 asserted that `repo-gates.yml` gains no install step, and specified
the mechanism — scan the lines matching `^ *run:`. It was run before the lock and
returned 0, so doctrine 7's amendment was satisfied. But `tests/run.sh` had
asserted that same invariant since F-14, by stripping comments and scanning
**every** remaining line — and its comment records that anchoring on `run:` was
tried and *rejected*, because `run: |` puts the install on its own line and
evades it. Implementing the locked criterion literally would have replaced a
working control with one that misses the ordinary multi-line form.

The criterion was invented rather than derived, because nobody grepped for the
invariant's existing owner. Cost here was one round trip and a spec correction;
the cost when the weaker check is the one that ships is a control everyone
believes in. **A criterion for an existing invariant should name and re-run the
existing assertion, not describe a fresh mechanism for it.** Where the new
wording and the shipped check disagree, assume the shipped check won its shape
the hard way and find out why before overruling it — the reason is usually
written next to it.

*Sharpening — an ITEM whose problem statement says "nothing checks X" is
making the same claim, and it is an absence claim, which is the kind that rots
silently.* **(W-40 retro, 2026-08-14.)** Doctrine 38 is written about criteria.
W-40 was filed saying the two config templates are hand-synced and "nothing
gates their agreement", naming only one check as insufficient. A second check
had existed since **the same commit that created the fork**, nine days before
the item was filed. Nobody had looked.

The item was still right, and for a worse reason than it claimed: the existing
check compares key *sets*, the drift changed a *value*, and it was **green
across the entire drift** — reproduced against the pre-fix upstream with a
clean baseline. So the finding inverted from "no gate exists" to "the gate
exists and is blind", which is a different item with a different fix.

The cost landed at the design panel. Sketches were handed the precedent for
*one* existing mechanism and not the other, and one duly proposed, as its
headline component, **a re-implementation of the check that already shipped** —
doctrine 38's exact defect, committed by a panel because the orchestrator gave
it an incomplete inventory. Two habits, both cheap: at Phase 2, treat every
"nothing checks X" in an item body as a claim to falsify with a grep before
drafting; and **hand the design panel the list of what already exists**, since
a sketch cannot avoid re-inventing a control it was never told about.

*Corollary — the mechanism you build to catch a class must cover all of that
class.* The same item added a `--anchors` dry pass so mutation patterns can be
re-verified in a second instead of a twenty-minute run (doctrine 33). It covered
the mutation table and **not** the compound-mutation anchors declared twenty
lines below it — so a compound anchor rotted when its target was rewritten, and
that rot surfaced only in a full run, which is precisely what the dry pass exists
to avoid. When you build a fast path for one shape of a check, enumerate the
other shapes before calling it done.

## Doctrine 66

**A criterion describing the state AFTER a PO-only action has never been
executed by anyone. It is a distinct class, and it is where the defects
are.** *(W-52 second rework, 2026-08-19 — an item was accepted and
un-accepted in the same session; five defects, all in that class.)*

Doctrine 7's W-28 amendment says run every command-shaped criterion before
the lock. Doctrine 20's W-47 amendment says execute *every* one, because the
skipped one is the one whose mechanism you assumed. W-52 obeyed both as far
as they can be obeyed — **fifteen criteria re-executed at the gate, fifteen
matched** — and was accepted on that evidence.

Two criteria could not be executed, by construction: they described the state
after *applying the guardrail queue*, which the guard reserves to the PO. The
PO applied. **Both were wrong**, and behind them were three more defects of
the same shape:

| | Coupled to the pre-apply world |
|---|---|
| the write-guard battery | rebuilt a patched copy unconditionally in 1 of its 4 sections — the patches cannot apply to a hook they are already in |
| a patch's currency probe | expected its own spec ID from a clause it merely *widened*, which denies in its original owner's name |
| the queue README | documented a staleness rule the gate did not implement |
| the mutation harness | asserted a baseline true only while an unapplied REPAIR sat past its SLA — so it refused to run at all, in the state the item exists to create |
| two harness fixtures | keyed on wording a sibling patch rewrites, so they read the neighbour's state, not their own |

The pattern is one thing, not five: **an artifact written while X is false
quietly assumes X is false**, and every gate you run before the action
confirms that assumption rather than testing it. A green suite here is not
weak evidence — it is *evidence about the other world*.

Note what makes this worse than ordinary staleness. The item's whole purpose
was to make the PO apply the queue. So the success condition and the
invalidating event **are the same event**, and the proof that the item worked
is destroyed by the item working.

Three habits, and the first is nearly free:

* **At lock time, mark every criterion whose truth depends on an action the
  session cannot take.** They are not ordinary criteria; they are
  *predictions*. Write them as predictions, and say what would falsify each.
* **Budget the first execution as part of the item, not as the confirmation
  that ends it.** Here it produced five defects, a reversed acceptance and a
  full rework — after the checklist had already gone fifteen-for-fifteen.
* **Ask of every artifact the item ships: does this still work once the
  action is taken?** Grep for the assumption rather than the file — anything
  that rebuilds, patches, or asserts "not yet applied" is a candidate. The
  write-guard battery had the correct pattern in three of four sections
  already (doctrine 62's call-site problem, in a harness).

Corollary worth its own line: **the refusal you add for one instance will
find the others.** The mis-aimed-probe refusal added for the second defect
immediately flagged two more, inside the harness's own fixtures — which is a
gate doing what doctrine 18 asks and being aimed at itself.

